      "id": 1,
      "text": "Eddyville was founded in 1798 by Revolutionary War veteran David Walker, who laid out the town at Eddy Creek and the Cumberland River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Eddyville,_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the swirling eddies in the nearby Cumberland River, a feature that early settlers found striking.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eddyville%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The penitentiary’s electric chair was used for public executions until 1936, reflecting a grim chapter in Kentucky’s legal history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eddyville%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Eddyville’s 1937 flood was one of the most devastating natural disasters in Lyon County, reshaping the community’s landscape.",
